#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:9
msgid "OpenBSD support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:11
msgid ""
"Starting from version 3.3.0 (released just now) psutil officially supports "
"OpenBSD. This was contributed by `Landry Breuil <https://github.com/"
"landryb>`__ and myself in :pr:`615`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:16
msgid "Differences with FreeBSD"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:18
msgid ""
"As expected, the OpenBSD implementation is very similar to FreeBSD's, so I "
"merged most of it into a single C file (`_psutil_bsd.c <https://github.com/"
"giampaolo/psutil/blob/release-3.3.0/psutil/_psutil_bsd.c>`__), with 2 "
"separate files (`freebsd.c <https://github.com/giampaolo/psutil/blob/"
"release-3.3.0/psutil/arch/bsd/freebsd.c>`__, `openbsd.c <https://github.com/"
"giampaolo/psutil/blob/release-3.3.0/psutil/arch/bsd/openbsd.c>`__) for the "
"parts that differ. Here are the functional differences with FreeBSD:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:26
msgid ""
":meth:`Process.memory_maps` is not implemented. The kernel provides the "
"necessary pieces but I haven't done this yet (hopefully later)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:28
msgid ""
":meth:`Process.num_ctx_switches`'s :field:`involuntary` field is always 0. "
"`kinfo_proc <https://github.com/giampaolo/psutil/blob/"
"fc1e59d08c968898c2ede425a621b62ccf44681c/psutil/_psutil_bsd.c#L335>`__ "
"provides this info but it is always set to 0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:31
msgid ":meth:`Process.cpu_affinity` (get and set) is not supported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:32
msgid ""
":meth:`Process.exe` is determined by inspecting the command line, so it may "
"not always be available (returns ``None``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:34
msgid ""
":func:`psutil.swap_memory` :field:`sin` and :field:`sout` (:term:`swap in "
"<swap-in>` and :term:`swap out <swap-out>`) values are not available, and "
"are therefore always set to 0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:37
msgid ":func:`psutil.cpu_count` ``(logical=False)`` always returns ``None``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:39
msgid ""
"As with FreeBSD, :meth:`Process.open_files` can't return file paths (FreeBSD "
"can sometimes). Otherwise everything is there and I'm satisfied with the "
"result."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:44
msgid "Considerations about BSD platforms"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:46
msgid ""
"psutil has supported FreeBSD since the beginning (year 2009). At the time, "
"it made sense to prefer it as the preferred BSD variant as it's the most "
"`most popular <https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/"
"Comparison_of_BSD_operating_systems#Popularity>`__."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:50
msgid ""
"Compared to FreeBSD, OpenBSD appears to be more \"minimal\", both in terms "
"of kernel facilities and the number of CLI tools available. One thing I "
"particularly appreciate about FreeBSD is that the source code for all CLI "
"tools is available under ``/usr/src``, which was a big help when "
"implementing psutil APIs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:56
msgid ""
"OpenBSD source code is `also available <https://github.com/openbsd/src>`__, "
"but it uses CVS and I am not sure it includes the source code for all CLI "
"tools."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:59
msgid ""
"There are still two more BSD variants worth supporting: NetBSD and "
"DragonFlyBSD (in this order). About a year ago, someone provided a patch (:"
"gh:`429`) adding basic NetBSD support, so that will likely happen sooner or "
"later."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:64
msgid "Other enhancements available in this release"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2015/openbsd-support.rst:66
msgid ""
"The only other enhancement is :gh:`558`, which allows specifying a different "
"location for the /proc filesystem on Linux."
msgstr ""

